// Package errors defines the standard Error interface — Binate's
// errors-as-values convention made concrete — plus the two canonical
// constructors, New (a leaf error carrying a message) and Wrap (a
// layer of context over an existing cause).
//
// Errors-as-values is the decided policy (no exceptions; errors are
// returned last in a tuple).  The "no error" value is the empty
// interface value: `var e @Error` constructs it, and `present(e)`
// tests whether a slot is filled.  A function reports failure as
// `func f(...) (T, @Error)` and callers branch on `present(err)`;
// there is no `nil` for interface values (see plan-std-errors.md and
// claude-notes.md on present/empty vs nil).
//
// Location rationale: this is a tier-1 convention, not a language
// primitive.  pkg/builtins/lang holds interfaces the language
// machinery itself consumes (Stringer ← print, Comparable ←
// dispatch); Error has no such language consumer, so it lives in a
// std package and is referenced as `@errors.Error` via import — the
// same honest cost as any other cross-package type.
package "pkg/std/errors"

// Error is the standard error interface.  An error renders a message
// for its layer and exposes the cause it wraps (if any) for
// programmatic walking.
//
// Unwrap is in the base interface deliberately — Rust's
// Error::source() model, not Go's optional-via-RTTI one.  A standard,
// mandatory accessor makes "add context, preserve the cause, stay
// programmatically walkable" the path of least resistance: a package
// that receives a dependency's error can wrap it with context without
// swallowing it, re-stringifying it, or leaking the dependency.  Walk
// a chain with `for present(e) { ...; e = e.Unwrap() }`.
//
// The parent is single (Unwrap() @Error), permanently — not
// @[]@Error.  Context-chaining (one parent, the common case) and
// multi-error aggregation (join) are different concerns; only the
// first belongs here.  Aggregation, if ever wanted, is a sibling
// opt-in interface, never a change to Error.
interface Error {
	// Error renders this error's message as a freshly-allocated
	// managed-slice the caller owns (mirroring Stringer.String()).  By
	// convention a wrapping error prepends its own context to the
	// cause's message ("context: cause"), so Error() on the outermost
	// error renders the whole chain.
	Error() @[]char

	// Unwrap returns the cause this error wraps, or an empty @Error
	// (test with `present`) for a leaf with no cause.
	Unwrap() @Error
}

// New returns a leaf Error carrying msg with no wrapped cause.  The
// returned error owns msg; its Unwrap() is an empty @Error.  A string
// literal works directly (`errors.New("file not found")`) — it is
// copied into the error's owned message.
func New(msg @[]char) @Error

// Wrap returns an Error that adds the context msg over cause: its
// Error() renders "msg: <cause's message>" and its Unwrap() returns
// cause.  The returned error owns both msg and the cause reference.
func Wrap(cause @Error, msg @[]char) @Error

// Is reports whether any error in err's Unwrap() chain is the same
// error object as target — the standard sentinel test, e.g.
// `errors.Is(err, io.EOF)`.  Because it walks the chain (err,
// err.Unwrap(), …, stopping at the empty @Error a leaf returns), it
// finds the sentinel even when it has been wrapped with context.  The
// comparison is reference identity (`same`): a layer matches target iff
// it is the very same boxed error.
func Is(err @Error, target @Error) bool
